return array (
  'content' => '<p>The rich patina that makes Corten desirable also produces iron oxide run-off during early weathering — particularly through the first heavy-rain cycles. Stained sandstone, white marble plinths and light concrete pavers below Corten can look neglected even when the metal itself is performing correctly. Mitigation is geometric and procedural, not a mystery chemical.</p><h2>Why run-off happens</h2><p>Until the patina stabilises, rain washes loose oxide particles off the surface. Vertical façades shed downward; canopies drip concentrated streams at edges. This is normal — not a sign of defective steel if detailing follows weathering steel guidance.</p><h2>Detailing prevention</h2><p>Drip grooves, outward-sloping sills and kerb gaps prevent run-off from tracking across sensitive stone. Avoid Corten overhangs directly above porous paving without drip edge throw.</p><p>Elevate planters and panel bases above finished grade on hidden supports so splash does not soak into tile joints.</p><p>Full façade guidance: <a href="/blog/corten-steel-facades-design-drainage-weathering">Corten façades design and drainage</a>.</p><h2>Landscape and paving sequencing</h2><p>Install final paving after Corten has initial weathering where programme allows — or mask and wash paving during first season. Light granite and limestone show stains fastest.</p><p>Dark basalt hides run-off better but is not a substitute for drip detail. Direct downpipes away from mixed-material junctions.</p><h2>Maintenance during maturation</h2><p>Gentle water rinse of stained paving early may reduce deep absorption — avoid aggressive acid cleaners on natural stone without specialist advice.</p><p>Do not wire-brush Corten to stop run-off — you reset patina and worsen short-term shedding.</p><h2>Material pairings</h2><p>Pair Corten with tolerant materials at ground zone — concrete, dark aggregate, gravel strips — and keep delicate stone higher or set back.</p><p>Upper façades in Corten above glass railings or <a href="/railings">stainless railings</a> reduce ground staining while keeping warmth at eye level.</p><h2>Project communication</h2><p>Tell clients run-off eases after patina matures — typically one to two monsoon cycles in many North site conditions, variable elsewhere.</p><p>Vyomika Atelier includes drip recommendations on shop drawings from our Delhi, India studio.</p><h2>Vertical façades versus horizontal planes</h2><p>Vertical Corten cladding sheds oxide downward in predictable bands. Horizontal soffits and planter edges hold moisture longer — run-off can drip for days after rain, concentrating stains beneath.</p><p>Slope horizontal Corten elements slightly or use drip noses. Where horizontal panels are unavoidable, increase buffer zone width below before sensitive paving begins.</p><h2>Temporary protection during construction</h2><p>Cover light paving with breathable geotextile during early weathering if handover must occur before patina stabilises. Remove covers after heavy rain cycles so panels continue wetting and drying naturally.</p><p>Do not apply permanent clear coats to stop run-off unless a specialist system is specified — ad-hoc sealants often fail unevenly and alter intended patina.</p><h2>Landscape architect coordination</h2><p>Set planting beds back from Corten plinths or use gravel transition strips. Iron oxide in soil is usually cosmetic but can discolour light gravel — specify dark aggregate in drip zones.</p><p>Pool decks and white marble courtyards need the strictest drip detail — coordinate with <a href="/blog/corten-steel-facades-design-drainage-weathering">drainage design</a> before civil works fix levels.</p><h2>When pre-weathering is worth considering</h2><p>Courtyards with no tolerance for early staining may specify pre-weathered panels from the fabricator yard. This shifts oxide shedding off site but adds cost and handling care to avoid scratching mature surfaces.</p><p>Interior Corten features weather slowly — run-off risk is minimal though occasional dusting may occur during first weeks.</p><h2>Canopy and soffit drip lines</h2><p>Entrance canopies in Corten create dramatic shadows but concentrate drip lines at outer edges. A hidden drip groove or stainless drip edge beneath the outer lip throws water away from light paving. Without it, a dark vertical stain can appear on stone cladding directly beneath the corner — even when the Corten itself is performing correctly.</p><p>Recessed downlights in Corten soffits need weep paths so water does not pool around fittings. Pooling prolongs wet contact and increases localized runoff when the panel finally drains.</p><h2>Renovation projects on existing paving</h2><p>Retrofit Corten screens on terraces with existing marble require earlier protection than new-build courtyards. Mask paving, plan temporary walkways, and schedule a post-monsoon rinse once patina begins to stabilize. Removing oxide stains from honed marble is specialist work — prevention remains cheaper.</p><h2>Owner maintenance expectations</h2><p>After patina stabilizes, runoff reduces sharply. Include a one-page owner note explaining the first-season behavior so facility teams do not attempt aggressive cleaning of the metal or acid washing of stone without guidance.</p><h2>Documenting drip details on shop drawings</h2><p>Mark drip groove locations, slope directions and minimum gap to paving on Corten shop drawings so site teams do not improvise during install. Landscape contractors should receive a highlighted plan zone showing where early runoff is expected during the first monsoon — temporary protection is easier when everyone expects it.</p><p>Link this document to the owner handover pack alongside photos of mature patina so future facility managers understand the difference between normal early staining and detailing failure.</p><h2>Inspection during first monsoon</h2><p>Walk the site after the first heavy rains to confirm drip paths behave as drawn. Note any unexpected pooling at panel bases and adjust landscape protection before subsequent storms. Early correction is faster than stone remediation later.</p><p>Photograph paving conditions at this stage for client records — visual proof that staining is easing helps maintain confidence while patina matures.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Reduce Corten run-off staining with drip edges, sensible paving sequence and realistic client briefing — not by fighting the weathering process. Plan ground-level materials to tolerate early oxide wash, then maintain paving lightly through the maturation period.</p><p>See <a href="/corten-steel">Corten steel</a>.</p><p>Understand patina in <a href="/blog/what-is-corten-steel-and-how-does-it-weather">how Corten weathers</a>.</p><p>Design façades via <a href="/blog/corten-steel-modern-facades">modern façade guide</a>.</p><p>For a project-specific quotation, share floor plans or photos through our <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. Architects and interior designers may also begin from the <a href="/professionals">professionals collaboration page</a>.</p>',
